  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Typical Summertime pattern continues with building \nheat next week. Storm chances today decreasing Sunday and Monday.\n\nEarlier showers and thunderstorms have dissipated. Primarily dry\nconditions are expected overnight. We'll continue to monitor\nstorms that are ongoing as of 915 PM across Ohio and western\nPennsylvania. This activity is expected to weaken and eventually\ndissipate as it moves toward the Allegheny Front after between\nMidnight and 3 AM. Temperatures are expected to remain mild\novernight, with overnight lows generally in the upper 60s to mid\n70s. \n\nA few storms may develop over the higher elevations on Sunday, \nbut coverage will be much less than in recent days. This will be\nshort-lived however, with storm chances increasing once again \nTuesday through the end of the week as a series of shortwave \ndisturbances pass through. \n\nThings have changed slightly on Monday, as precipitation \nchances have increased a bit over the past several model runs. \nAnother piece of shortwave energy will slide across the region \namidst a hot and very humid airmass yet again. Similar to what \nwe are seeing today, coverage is a question mark at this point, \nbut could be a couple of pretty strong to severe storms that \nday. Something that bears watching at this stage, but confidence\nis just too low for a severe weather outlook at this point. \n\nOutside of he storm chances expect highs in the upper 80s and low \n90s with heat indices east of the Blue Ridge up around 100 degrees. \nThe hottest days look to be Monday and Tuesday given the \nstrengthened subtropical ridge over the region. Highs will push into \nthe mid 90s with heat indices closer to 105 degrees east of the Blue \nRidge/along the urban corridor.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front could arrive late next week bringing \nrelief from the heat and humidity into the upcoming weekend. \n\nDaily storm chances look to continue through at least Friday as a \nmore potent frontal system tracks toward the region. Ahead of that \nfront will be several fast moving shortwave disturbances which will \nhelp aid in shower and thunderstorm development during the diurnal \nperiod. Storm coverage is a bit uncertain with activity remaining \nisolated to scattered Tuesday becoming a scattered to numerous \nWednesday into late week as the front tries to push through. \nSpeaking of the front, details remain a bit murky on it's passage. \nMost 00z ensemble/deterministic guidance suggests a frontal passage \nThursday afternoon into Saturday. Guidance also depicts an MCS type \nsetup during the Tuesday and Wednesday timeframe. Machine learning \nguidance is grabbing onto this as well with probabilities running \nbetween 15 to 30 percent for severe weather during the midweek \ntimeframe. SPC mentions areas back toward our west on the Day 4 and \n5 outlook, so something we'll continue to monitor, especially as the \nsubtropical ridge flattens. \n\nThere is a bit more uncertainty in regards to severe weather \nThursday through Saturday. Of course with any decent frontal passage \nwill have to be monitor given machine probabilities remaining \nbetween 5-30 percent for severe weather. This will ultimately be \ndetermined by the timing of the front in regards to the peak heating \nperiod. Temperatures will cool from the 90s into the 80s late week. \nWeather does look to improve for the upcoming weekend as Canadian \nhigh pressure works into the region.",
